5.  L9302-L9305 -- §E.1.4 and §29.2.2
================================================================================================

  §E.1.4 at L11033, body to L11059
  --- §E.1.4 L11033-L11059 ---
   11033 ### E.1.4 This is an index of tractable ignorance, and it cannot say otherwise
   11034  Every item here is closable. The obstacle coordinate takes three values — **nonexistent** (the datum
   11035  can be made to exist), **buildable** (it can be constructed), **retrievable** (it can be fetched) —
   11036  and all three name a route. Eleven items: one nonexistent, two buildable, eight retrievable.
   11037 
   11038     **No value of this coordinate means *there is no route*.** An unclosable question is not
   11039     missing from this appendix. It is **inexpressible** in the coordinates the appendix uses.
   11040 
   11041  **That is not an oversight, and §32.1.4 says why it cannot be repaired from inside.** ℛ(X) ⊆ ∏ᵢ Aᵢ(X):
   11042  the operator is bounded by the index's own value sets, so it can name a cell the index lacks and never
   11043  a *value*. Nothing in this appendix could ever produce the fourth obstacle, however long it ran.
   11044 
   11045  **Offer it from outside and the shape of what is missing appears.** Adding *no route* as a fourth
   11046  obstacle value grows the ambient box from 18 cells to 24 and takes E from 4 to **10** — six further
   11047  combinations the structure admits and this appendix does not contain, every one of them an unclosable
   11048  question at some cost and some consequence.
   11049 
   11050  **So the book has two kinds of ignorance and indexes only one.** The tractable kind is here, graded and
   11051  costed. The other kind is elsewhere and unindexed: §29.2.2's reading that the branches leave the
   11052  object, and the destination coordinate that the temporal mechanisms would need. **Those are not open
   11053  items. They are the shape of the question this index cannot pose**, and each had to be named in prose
   11054  because no coordinate here would hold it.
   11055 
   11056  That is register 332 arriving where it is least comfortable — an index names cells and never
   11057  coordinates, so **an index of one's own ignorance is an index of the ignorance one already has words
   11058  for.** Register 382.
   11059 

  §29.2.2 at L7927, body to L7944
  --- §29.2.2 L7927-L7944 ---
   7927 ### 29.2.2 A reading the language cannot close
   7928  The promotion arithmetic of §12.11.0.10 invites a reading in which the branches leave the object and the
   7929  lattice continues into indices we cannot observe. **It is recorded here and not argued**, because P20
   7930  says what kind of claim it is.
   7931 
   7932  A question closes at a cost set by the language it is posed in: monotone two-variable bounds close in
   7933  one pass; full arithmetic never closes, by Gödel; **the documentary language has no closure mechanism
   7934  at all.** A claim about indices outside every measurement is posed in the third. It cannot be settled
   7935  in either direction by any search, which is the same status §29.1 proves for novelty — **not false,
   7936  not true, unclosable** — and the discipline is identical: name it, decline to argue it, and do not let
   7937  it lean on results that were computed inside.
   7938 
   7939  **What was computed stands and does not support it.** Λ₁₃ enters a larger index at a cost of 12.14
   7940  bits per cell; a coarse coordinate branches up to 185 ways; composition seen from above is an interval
   7941  and not a map. All three are statements about *this* object viewed at lower resolution. **None of them
   7942  reaches outside it, and §32.1.4 proves none of them can**: ℛ(X) ⊆ ∏ᵢ Aᵢ(X), so the operator cannot
   7943  produce a value the index lacks, let alone an axis, let alone another index. Register 335.
   7944 

